      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "The lipophilic isoquinoline carboxamide PET tracer PK11195 exhibits low brain uptake and impermeable BBB behavior, showing that lipophilic heteroaromatic carboxamides can still have poor CNS penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "The simple sulfonamide NBBS showed low brain:plasma ratios in rats and mice, directly demonstrating that sulfonamide-containing molecules can have poor BBB penetration; the query contains a methanesulfonamide NH.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "m-AMSA, which contains a methanesulfonamide anilide and a large fused aromatic system, achieved CSF concentrations only 1-3% of systemic concentrations in a primate model, supporting poor CNS access for the query's sulfonamide/aromatic scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "KSG-504, a sulfonamide-containing drug, was not detected in rabbit CSF after intravenous administration, indicating poor central permeability and supporting a fail prediction for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "All retrieved experimental analogues with high assay-transfer scores demonstrate poor brain/plasma ratios, low CSF penetration, or impermeable BBB; the query's sulfonamide/amide HBDs and lipophilic heteroaromatic core align with these liabilities, so meaningful CNS access is unlikely.",
